From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timon Stipkovits <timon2201@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] entry: Make allowed syscalls cheaper with Syscall User
 Dispatch armed

Once a task arms Syscall User Dispatch, every one of its syscalls goes
through syscall_user_dispatch(), and for Wine that is every thread and
almost every syscall, nearly all of them allowed by the selector.  For
each allowed native 64-bit syscall the function currently makes two
more calls:

 - arch_syscall_is_vdso_sigreturn(), out of line in arch/x86/entry/vdso,
   which can only return true for ia32 syscalls;
 - __get_user() for the selector byte, i.e. __get_user_nocheck_1, which
   executes an LFENCE (ASM_BARRIER_NOSPEC) before the load (7.3; 7.4
   already uses get_user(), which masks the address instead, but still
   calls the out-of-line helper).

Read the selector first, inline, with scoped_user_read_access() and
unsafe_get_user(): a masked access without a call or a speculation
barrier.  If it says ALLOW, nothing else needs checking.  The vDSO
sigreturn check now runs only for syscalls that are not allowed, and
before a selector fault is turned into SIGSEGV, so every combination of
selector state and sigreturn gives the same result as before (the
decision table in extras/sud-tests/test_sud.c checks all of them
against both orders).

An LFENCE costs ~5 ns in a userspace loop on a Xeon; with the SRSO
"safe RET" mitigation (Zen 1-4) each of the two calls also goes through
the return thunk.  Armed allowed syscalls measured 10-15 ns slower than
unarmed ones on 6.18 before this change.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 kernel/entry/syscall_user_dispatch.c | 45 ++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/kernel/entry/syscall_user_dispatch.c b/kernel/entry/syscall_user_dispatch.c
index 12575db9f..28b1e33ab 100644
--- a/kernel/entry/syscall_user_dispatch.c
+++ b/kernel/entry/syscall_user_dispatch.c
@@ -110,27 +110,46 @@ bool syscall_user_dispatch(struct pt_regs *regs)
 {
 	struct syscall_user_dispatch *sd = &current->syscall_dispatch;
-	char state;
+	char __user *selector = sd->selector;
+	char state = SYSCALL_DISPATCH_FILTER_BLOCK;
 
 	if (likely(instruction_pointer(regs) - sd->offset < sd->len))
 		return false;
 
-	if (unlikely(arch_syscall_is_vdso_sigreturn(regs)))
-		return false;
-
-	if (likely(sd->selector)) {
-		if (unlikely(get_user(state, untagged_addr(sd->selector)))) {
-			force_exit_sig(SIGSEGV);
-			return true;
-		}
+	/*
+	 * This runs on every syscall of a task with dispatch armed, and the
+	 * selector says ALLOW for most of them, so read it first: then
+	 * nothing else needs checking.  The read is inline and masked
+	 * instead of a call to the __get_user() helper and its speculation
+	 * barrier (LFENCE on x86), and the vDSO sigreturn check, a call into
+	 * arch code, only runs for syscalls that are not allowed.  The
+	 * outcome is the same for every combination as with the checks in
+	 * their old order.
+	 */
+	if (likely(selector)) {
+		selector = untagged_addr(selector);
+		scoped_user_read_access(selector, efault)
+			unsafe_get_user(state, selector, efault);
 
 		if (likely(state == SYSCALL_DISPATCH_FILTER_ALLOW))
 			return false;
+	}
 
-		if (state != SYSCALL_DISPATCH_FILTER_BLOCK) {
-			force_exit_sig(SIGSYS);
-			return true;
-		}
+	if (unlikely(arch_syscall_is_vdso_sigreturn(regs)))
+		return false;
+
+	if (selector && state != SYSCALL_DISPATCH_FILTER_BLOCK) {
+		force_exit_sig(SIGSYS);
+		return true;
 	}
+	goto dispatch;
+
+efault:
+	/* The selector can't be read: as before, a vDSO sigreturn still passes. */
+	if (unlikely(arch_syscall_is_vdso_sigreturn(regs)))
+		return false;
+	force_exit_sig(SIGSEGV);
+	return true;
 
+dispatch:
 	sd->on_dispatch = true;
 	syscall_rollback(current, regs);
